ing for  close to two dollars but for a  population living in extreme poverty  that can feel like a death sentence  and that was made evident in this viral  video showing a shopper's visceral  reaction at a supermarket to the  increased food prices  on the streets the view is similar  you can't expect someone earning a  dollar a day to buy maize flour for two  dollars children here are going to  school hungry we can't even feed them  breakfast  food security expert anne miner has been  critical of african government's  inability to create crop and diet  diversity her suggestion focus on  climate resilient and nature-driven  farming we need to harness our resources  already we have several huge drums that  have been built but also putting money  in investing in small irrigation  facilities conservation of water  building of water plants would make a  huge difference in terms of food  production in kenya  back in the rift valley jonathan is  already rolling out his next plans  sorghum a more drought resistant crop  with a ready market he's hopeful he'll  bounce back  when the rains come  if they come edith uh tell me about it  just just how bad is the situation where  you are  it's more obvious in the northeastern  part of the country which has a more  arid and semi-arid climate and there a  food security steering in the steering  group in the country estimates that 3.7  million people are facing starvation in  the cities where i am or in the  breadbasket regions like where i made  that report from it's less obvious but  the climate shocks are definitely  affecting the harvest as we saw in the  report but also a serious pest issue we  were being told in 2020 that kenya is  losing as much maize to pests as it is  as it is importing so there's a huge  problem uh with food supplies not to  mention the global commodity shops that  we're seeing of course because of the  war in ukraine  is there a reason heat resilient  varieties can't be used across the whole  country  the first reason is economic as a small  scale farmer it makes more sense for me  to grow maize because i can then offload  it quite easily to the people around me  if i'm using something like sogam or  millet which is less of a staple in the  country it means i have to have a  contract with say a brewery and that's  less uh  then of course there's access uh the  people who do want to use these draft  resistance seeds don't have access to  them because they'll be available in the  country as of 2023 but only in an  experimental capacity and this is what  we got from the ministry and finally  there's the health concern particularly  biodiversity and biosafety groups here  in the country are asking why would we  want to invest in genetically modified  crops when in the west  the move is more organic  edith camani thank you very much for  your reporting  you
 

